WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.830
Adding a key to a search tree was easy

2
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.330
walk down, and attach a new leaf.

3
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.260
Nothing else had to move.

4
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.562
Removing a key is harder

5
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.815
because the key may sit in

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.160
the middle of the tree

7
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.050
with whole subtrees hanging below it.

8
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.171
How do we take a node out and still

9
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.115
keep the rule that left is smaller

10
00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.760
and right is larger?

11
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.859
The answer depends on how many

12
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.660
children the node has.

13
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.001
In this video we'll see the three cases

14
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.717
why the in-order successor

15
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.380
is the right replacement

16
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.010
and what deletion costs.

17
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.320
Case one: the node is a leaf.

18
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.500
Take twenty.

19
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.540
It has no children

20
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.270
so nothing hangs below it.

21
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.050
We just cut it off

22
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.890
the left child of thirty becomes empty.

23
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.110
The ordering rule still holds

24
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.140
because we removed a key and moved nothing.

25
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.910
Case two: the node has one child.

26
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.430
Take forty.

27
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.040
Its only child is forty-five.

28
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.220
Forty hangs below thirty.

29
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.656
We can let forty-five take its place

30
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.714
the parent of forty now points

31
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.100
to forty-five directly

32
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.550
and forty simply drops out of the tree.

33
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.190
Is the rule still true?

34
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.180
Forty-five was in the right

35
00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.530
subtree of thirty

36
00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.470
so it is larger than thirty

37
00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.750
and smaller than fifty.

38
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:27.710
Yes.

39
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.610
A whole subtree moves up one level

40
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.060
and its order inside is unchanged.

41
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.630
Case three: the node has two children.

42
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.046
Take the root, fifty.

43
00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.786
Its left subtree now holds

44
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.050
thirty and forty-five

45
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.970
and its right subtree holds seventy, sixty

46
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.880
sixty-five and eighty.

47
00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.053
You might think of just removing fifty and

48
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.180
hanging both subtrees from its parent.

49
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.188
But the root has no parent

50
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.470
and a node in the middle

51
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.940
has only one free slot

52
00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.200
with two subtrees to put in it.

53
00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.710
That does not work.

54
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.290
So we do something different.

55
00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.161
We do not remove the node.

56
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.594
We replace its key with another

57
00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.050
key from the tree

58
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.500
and remove that other node instead.

59
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.012
Which key can take fifty's place

60
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.813
without breaking the rule?

61
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.977
It must be larger than everything

62
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.499
on the left, and smaller than everything

63
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.810
else on the right.

64
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.710
There are two candidates

65
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.350
the largest key on the left

66
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.012
or the smallest key on the right.

67
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.913
The smallest key on the right is

68
00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.770
called the in-order successor

69
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.025
because in the sorted order of the

70
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.400
keys it comes right after fifty.

71
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.080
To find it, go right once

72
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.560
and then left as far as you can.

73
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.230
Here that is sixty.

74
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.530
Copy sixty into the root.

75
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.291
Now the tree has sixty twice

76
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.590
so remove the old sixty from

77
00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.730
the right subtree.

78
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.050
And that is easy

79
00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.030
the successor never has a left child

80
00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.690
so it is a case one or a case two.

81
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.030
Here it has one child, sixty-five

82
00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.300
which takes its place.

83
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.240
The root is now sixty

84
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.740
and the walk in order gives thirty

85
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.180
forty-five, sixty, sixty-five, seventy

86
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.020
eighty.

87
00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.290
Still sorted.

88
00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.180
How long does deletion take?

89
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.060
First we search for the key.

90
00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.940
That is one path down from the root

91
00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.270
so at most h steps, where h is the height.

92
00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.701
In the third case we also walk

93
00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.050
down to the successor

94
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.415
but that continues the same path downward

95
00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.902
so the total never goes deeper

96
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:28.835
than the height.

97
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.240
Everything else is changing

98
00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.540
one or two links.

99
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.180
So delete, like search and insert

100
00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.890
takes time O of h.

101
00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.661
To sum up.

102
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.230
Deleting a node from a search

103
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.960
tree has three cases.

104
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.886
A leaf is simply cut off.

105
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.688
A node with one child is

106
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.710
replaced by that child.

107
00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.090
A node with two children takes the

108
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.090
key of its in-order successor

109
00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.630
the smallest key of its right subtree

110
00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.090
and then that successor node is removed.

111
00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.089
All three cost time proportional

112
00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.260
to the height.

113
00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.800
In the last video of this chapter

114
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.850
what if the height is not small?

115
00:04:06.477 --> 00:04:09.157
Now let's write delete in Java.

116
00:04:09.737 --> 00:04:12.395
First, a helper that changes nothing.

117
00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:14.207
It walks down to the node and

118
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:17.127
reports which case applies: leaf

119
00:04:17.127 --> 00:04:19.167
one child or two children

120
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:20.937
or null if the key is missing.

121
00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:23.907
Delete is written recursively.

122
00:04:23.987 --> 00:04:25.528
The method returns the new

123
00:04:25.587 --> 00:04:26.907
root of the subtree

124
00:04:26.907 --> 00:04:28.837
so that the caller can store it.

125
00:04:29.187 --> 00:04:31.077
If the key is smaller

126
00:04:31.077 --> 00:04:33.457
we assign the result to the left child.

127
00:04:33.457 --> 00:04:36.297
If it is larger, to the right child.

128
00:04:36.297 --> 00:04:38.307
Otherwise we found the node.

129
00:04:38.887 --> 00:04:40.628
One Java detail.

130
00:04:40.737 --> 00:04:42.814
The recursive method must

131
00:04:42.897 --> 00:04:44.322
return the subtree

132
00:04:44.397 --> 00:04:46.223
but we also want to report whether

133
00:04:46.277 --> 00:04:48.057
a key was actually removed.

134
00:04:48.057 --> 00:04:50.437
Java cannot return two values from a method

135
00:04:50.437 --> 00:04:52.517
so we keep a boolean field

136
00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:54.097
set it when we find the node

137
00:04:54.097 --> 00:04:55.607
and read it afterwards.

138
00:04:55.957 --> 00:04:59.597
The size goes down only if the flag is set.

139
00:05:00.177 --> 00:05:02.627
No left child: return the right child.

140
00:05:02.627 --> 00:05:04.946
No right child: return the left child.

141
00:05:05.007 --> 00:05:06.865
That covers all the leaves and

142
00:05:06.927 --> 00:05:08.217
the single-child nodes.

143
00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:11.430
With two children, we find the minimum node

144
00:05:11.497 --> 00:05:12.917
of the right subtree

145
00:05:12.917 --> 00:05:15.377
copy its key into this node

146
00:05:15.377 --> 00:05:18.287
and delete that key from the right subtree.

147
00:05:18.867 --> 00:05:20.117
Running the demo

148
00:05:20.117 --> 00:05:22.737
we get the same steps as in the first part

149
00:05:22.737 --> 00:05:24.437
a leaf, one child

150
00:05:24.437 --> 00:05:26.717
two children with the root becoming sixty

151
00:05:26.717 --> 00:05:29.227
and a missing key returning false.

152
00:05:29.577 --> 00:05:31.387
After every step

153
00:05:31.387 --> 00:05:34.017
isBst says the tree is ordered.

154
00:05:34.597 --> 00:05:37.027
Let's trace the deletion of fifty

155
00:05:37.027 --> 00:05:38.427
the root with two children.

156
00:05:38.427 --> 00:05:40.667
The first call finds the key at the root.

157
00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:43.517
It has two children, so the flag is set.

158
00:05:43.867 --> 00:05:45.752
The successor is the smallest

159
00:05:45.817 --> 00:05:48.717
key in the right subtree, sixty.

160
00:05:48.717 --> 00:05:50.617
We copy it into the root

161
00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:53.617
and call delete again on the right subtree

162
00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:54.867
with the key sixty.

163
00:05:55.217 --> 00:05:57.587
That call goes left at seventy

164
00:05:57.587 --> 00:06:00.187
and the next call finds the old sixty.

165
00:06:00.187 --> 00:06:01.967
It has no left child

166
00:06:01.967 --> 00:06:05.177
so it returns its right child, sixty-five.

167
00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:07.737
Seventy now points to sixty-five

168
00:06:07.737 --> 00:06:09.777
and the calls return upward.

169
00:06:09.777 --> 00:06:12.197
Three calls, one per level

170
00:06:12.197 --> 00:06:14.247
never deeper than the height.

171
00:06:14.827 --> 00:06:16.437
Common mistakes.

172
00:06:16.437 --> 00:06:19.517
First, ignoring the returned subtree

173
00:06:19.517 --> 00:06:21.507
so the change is lost.

174
00:06:21.857 --> 00:06:24.850
Second, deleting by the old key instead

175
00:06:24.927 --> 00:06:26.377
of the successor's key.

176
00:06:26.727 --> 00:06:29.032
Third, deep recursion.

177
00:06:29.137 --> 00:06:32.706
One call per level means that a chain of a million

178
00:06:32.777 --> 00:06:35.717
nodes throws a StackOverflowError.

179
00:06:35.717 --> 00:06:38.457
For normal trees the height is small

180
00:06:38.457 --> 00:06:39.807
and the recursion is fine.

181
00:06:40.387 --> 00:06:42.668
TreeMap handles all this for you.

182
00:06:42.737 --> 00:06:44.533
Its remove method is exactly

183
00:06:44.597 --> 00:06:47.413
these three cases, plus the rebalancing that

184
00:06:47.477 --> 00:06:48.397
keeps the tree short

185
00:06:48.397 --> 00:06:49.707
which is the next chapter.

186
00:06:50.287 --> 00:06:52.378
In Java: three cases

187
00:06:52.477 --> 00:06:54.196
a recursive method returning

188
00:06:54.257 --> 00:06:57.197
the new subtree, a flag for the answer

189
00:06:57.197 --> 00:06:58.887
and the successor trick.

190
00:06:59.237 --> 00:07:03.117
Next: when do search trees go wrong?

